<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-belarus">Brest-Litovsk</a>, signed on 3 March 1918 between Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, took Russia out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Russia gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the Baltic lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transcaucasian-sejm">Transcaucasian Sejm</a> towards independence. The treaty was annulled after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in November 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk.jpg" alt="Treaty of Brest-Litovsk" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traktat_brzeski_1918.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 1917 the Eastern Front was stalemated and the Russian economy had nearly collapsed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> forced Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> to abdicate, but the Provisional Government continued the war. After the Red Guards seized the Winter Palace on 7 November in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>, the new government at once issued a Decree on Peace. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> was concluded on 15 December 1917, and negotiations opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-belarus">Brest-Litovsk</a> on 22 December.</p><p>The Soviet delegation was led first by Adolph Joffe, with Max Hoffmann, Richard von Kühlmann, Ottokar Czernin and Talaat Pasha among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>' negotiators. The Soviet side demanded peace with no annexations or indemnities; the Central Powers accepted this only if all belligerents did, and Hoffmann then made clear that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, already occupied, would separate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> replaced Joffe, since the Soviets hoped to prolong the talks until revolutions broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> on 8–9 February 1918. Trotsky's formula of 'no war, no peace' was announced on 10 February, but on 18 February 53 German divisions advanced against near-empty Soviet trenches. Lenin argued that the Soviets must sign or face an even more ruinous treaty, telling the Central Committee that they must concede space to win time.</p><p>After Hoffmann presented harsher terms on 23 February, the Central Committee backed signing, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-russian-central-executive-committee">All-Russian Central Executive Committee</a> passed it by 116 to 85, with the Left Socialist-Revolutionaries jeering Lenin as a traitor. Grigori Sokolnikov signed on 3 March 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> renounced territorial claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, Latvia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a> were to be cleared of Russian troops. It lost 89% of its coalfields and 26% of its railways, and Kotkin writes that no Russian government had ever surrendered so much territory or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. A supplementary agreement of 27 August 1918 obliged Russia to pay six billion marks.</p><p>The occupation proved costly for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with over one million troops staying in the east. The treaty split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> from the Left SRs, who assassinated the German ambassador in July 1918. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice of 11 November 1918</a> abrogated the treaty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the Baltic states re-emerged, and at Rapallo in 1922 Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> renounced all wartime claims.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Brest-Litovsk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b> of March 1918, Russia, by then in civil war, ceded all the Polish lands it had held to the German Empire and Austria-Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1918 the government moved the capital from Petrograd to Moscow and signed the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b> with the Central Powers, giving up Poland, the Baltic lands, Finland and Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>That republic was dissolved after the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b>, when the Bolsheviks were forced out of Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b>, German and Austro-Hungarian forces advanced into the Donbas.</blockquote><a href="/state/donetsk-krivoy-rog-soviet-republic">Donetsk-Krivoy Rog Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b> in March 1918, Germany allowed client institutions, including the Lithuanian Taryba, which had declared independence in February 1918, and planned a Duchy of Courland and Semigallia (1918).</blockquote><a href="/state/ober-ost">Ober Ost</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b>, Austro-Hungarian and German troops advanced on Odessa, and the Soviet leaders evacuated the city in March 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/odessa-soviet-republic">Odessa Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
